1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,827
This is STAT 331, video 10 a. The topic is the prediction of a new response.

2
00:00:07,277 --> 00:00:13,741
This video prepares you for three question types. Each type gets a method card and worked problems.

3
00:00:14,191 --> 00:00:21,145
Type A: derive the prediction interval. Lecture 4 does this on slides 31 to 37.

4
00:00:21,595 --> 00:00:30,235
Type B: compute a predicted value and a prediction interval from computer output, as in Tutorial 2, question 1 e.

5
00:00:30,685 --> 00:00:37,640
Type C: compare a confidence interval with a prediction interval, and decide if you can trust a prediction.

6
00:00:38,090 --> 00:00:45,834
Here are the brainhead data from the slides. There are 236 people. The green line is the fitted line.

7
00:00:46,284 --> 00:00:54,689
Take a new person with head size x new equal to 4200. As always, we treat x new as fixed.

8
00:00:55,139 --> 00:01:03,822
In video 9, we estimated the mean response mu 0 at this x. The purple dot is this mean. It is a fixed number.

9
00:01:04,272 --> 00:01:19,952
Now we want the brain weight of this one new person. The model gives y new equals beta 0, plus beta 1 x new, plus epsilon new. The orange curve shows the normal spread of y new, with variance sigma squared.

10
00:01:20,402 --> 00:01:32,029
The predicted value is y new hat. It equals beta 0 hat plus beta 1 hat times x new. This is the same number as mu 0 hat, the green dot.

11
00:01:32,479 --> 00:01:43,337
So the point estimate does not change. But one new y also has its own error, epsilon new. The interval must include this additional uncertainty.

12
00:01:43,787 --> 00:01:50,337
Why is there additional uncertainty? The gray dashed line is the true line. We do not know it.

13
00:01:50,787 --> 00:02:01,496
Source 1. Each sample gives different estimates. Here are the fitted lines of 9 simulated samples. So y new hat changes from sample to sample.

14
00:02:01,946 --> 00:02:10,031
Source 2. The new response has its own error, epsilon new. Even with the true line, y new is not on the line.

15
00:02:10,481 --> 00:02:17,137
The green bar shows the spread of y new hat, from source 1. Video 9 measured this spread.

16
00:02:17,587 --> 00:02:26,995
The orange bar shows the spread of y new, with variance sigma squared. For the brainhead data, it is much larger than the green bar.

17
00:02:27,445 --> 00:02:30,816
So a prediction interval must include both sources.

18
00:02:31,266 --> 00:02:40,375
Section 2. Question type A asks you to derive the prediction interval. Lecture 4 does it on slides 31 to 37.

19
00:02:40,825 --> 00:02:48,420
Method card A has five steps. Step 1: write the prediction error, y new hat minus y new, with the model.

20
00:02:48,870 --> 00:02:56,934
Step 2: find its mean. Use the facts that the estimators are unbiased, and that epsilon new has mean 0.

21
00:02:57,384 --> 00:03:03,144
Step 3: find its variance. The two parts are independent, so their variances add.

22
00:03:03,594 --> 00:03:13,279
Step 4: standardize. The error is normal, so we get a standard normal. Then replace sigma with sigma hat, and we get a t distribution.

23
00:03:13,729 --> 00:03:21,900
Step 5: write the probability that T is between the two t quantiles. Then solve the inequality for y new.

24
00:03:22,350 --> 00:03:28,622
The next three slides do these steps. The result is the formula that question type B uses.

25
00:03:29,072 --> 00:03:48,507
Here is the derivation of slides 31 to 33. Pause the video and try it. Step 1 of card A: define the prediction error, y new hat minus y new. Both parts are random. The green curve is the distribution of y new hat. Its centre is mu 0.

26
00:03:48,957 --> 00:04:06,663
Write both parts with the model. y new hat is beta 0 hat plus beta 1 hat x new. y new is beta 0, plus beta 1 x new, plus epsilon new. The orange curve is the distribution of y new. Its centre is also mu 0.

27
00:04:07,113 --> 00:04:18,185
Step 2 of card A: the mean. The expected value of a sum is the sum of the expected values. x new is fixed, so it comes out of the expected value.

28
00:04:18,635 --> 00:04:30,625
The estimators are unbiased. The expected value of beta 0 hat is beta 0, and of beta 1 hat is beta 1. And epsilon new has mean 0.

29
00:04:31,075 --> 00:04:39,437
The two brackets are the same, so they cancel. The purple curve is the distribution of the prediction error. Its centre is 0.

30
00:04:39,887 --> 00:04:47,162
So on average, the prediction is right. Question type B uses y new hat as the centre of the interval.

31
00:04:47,612 --> 00:04:57,191
Pause the video and try it. Step 3 of card A: the variance. Write y new hat as beta 0 hat plus beta 1 hat x new.

32
00:04:57,641 --> 00:05:14,601
Why are y new hat and y new independent? y new hat uses only y 1 to y n, so only epsilon 1 to epsilon n. y new uses only epsilon new. The errors are independent, so no arrow crosses the red line.

33
00:05:15,051 --> 00:05:28,085
For independent random variables, the variance of a difference is the sum of the variances. The minus sign does not matter, because the variance of minus Y equals the variance of Y.

34
00:05:28,535 --> 00:05:51,511
The first variance is from video 9. It is sigma squared, times 1 over n plus x new minus x bar squared over S x x. For the brainhead data at x new equal to 3000, this bracket is 0.0042 plus 0.0133, which is 0.0175.

35
00:05:51,961 --> 00:06:02,009
The second variance is sigma squared, because the only random part of y new is epsilon new. In the picture, it adds a bar of length 1.

36
00:06:02,459 --> 00:06:20,273
Take sigma squared out as a common factor. The bracket is 1 plus 1 over n plus the x term. For the example, 1 plus 0.0175 is 1.0175. The 1 is about 98 percent of it.

37
00:06:20,723 --> 00:06:27,229
This is the variance of the prediction error. Question type B computes this bracket in its step 3.

38
00:06:27,679 --> 00:06:43,317
Pause the video and try it. Step 4 of card A. The prediction error is a linear function of the normal y i and epsilon new, so it is normal. Its mean is 0, and its variance is sigma squared times the bracket.

39
00:06:43,767 --> 00:06:55,479
Subtract the mean 0 and divide by the standard deviation. The result has a standard normal distribution. On the right, the axis is now in units of 1.

40
00:06:55,929 --> 00:07:18,435
But sigma is unknown. Replace sigma with sigma hat. The logic is the same as for beta 1 hat. n minus 2, times sigma hat squared over sigma squared, is chi squared with n minus 2 degrees of freedom, and it is independent of the top. So T has a t distribution with n minus 2 degrees of freedom.

41
00:07:18,885 --> 00:07:35,995
Step 5 of card A. With probability 1 minus alpha, T is between minus t and plus t. Here t is the 1 minus alpha over 2 quantile of t with n minus 2 degrees of freedom. The green area is 1 minus alpha.

42
00:07:36,445 --> 00:07:47,559
Each red tail has area alpha over 2. Multiply the inequality by sigma hat times the root. Then move y new hat, and y new is in the middle.

43
00:07:48,009 --> 00:08:04,820
So the 100 times 1 minus alpha percent prediction interval is y new hat, plus or minus t, times sigma hat, times the root of 1 plus 1 over n plus the x term. Question type B uses this formula.

44
00:08:05,270 --> 00:08:17,259
Section 3. Question type B asks for a predicted value and a prediction interval at a new x. Tutorial 2, question 1 e, gives R output and asks this.

45
00:08:17,709 --> 00:08:27,395
Step 1 of method card B: read n, x bar, S x x, the two estimates, and sigma hat. They come from the output or from the data.

46
00:08:27,845 --> 00:08:34,714
Step 2: the predicted value. y new hat equals beta 0 hat plus beta 1 hat x new.

47
00:08:35,164 --> 00:08:42,545
Step 3: the bracket. 1 plus 1 over n plus x new minus x bar squared over S x x.

48
00:08:42,995 --> 00:08:48,435
Step 4: the standard error. It is sigma hat times the square root of the bracket.

49
00:08:48,885 --> 00:08:59,296
Step 5: find the t quantile with n minus 2 degrees of freedom. The interval is y new hat plus or minus t times the standard error.

50
00:08:59,746 --> 00:09:15,255
Now a tutorial question of type B. Pause the video and try it. Tutorial 2, question 1 e: predict the satisfaction of a new patient with severity 55, with a 95 percent prediction interval.

51
00:09:15,705 --> 00:09:41,817
Step 1 of card B: read the values. n is 25. x bar is 50.48. S x x is 532.24. Beta 0 hat is 179.1312, and beta 1 hat is minus 2.3283. Sigma hat is the residual standard error, 13.52, on 23 degrees of freedom.

52
00:09:42,267 --> 00:10:05,115
Step 2: y new hat equals 179.1312 minus 2.3283 times 55. 2.3283 times 55 is 128.0565, so y new hat is 51.07. The green dot on the number line shows it.

53
00:10:05,565 --> 00:10:22,739
Step 3: the x term. 55 minus 50.48 is 4.52. 4.52 squared is 20.43. Divide by 532.24, and the x term is 0.0384.

54
00:10:23,189 --> 00:10:39,530
Now the bracket. 1, plus 1 over 25, which is 0.04, plus 0.0384. That is 1.0784. The bar shows the three parts. The 1 is the largest part.

55
00:10:39,980 --> 00:10:55,212
Step 4: the standard error is 13.52 times the square root of 1.0784. The square root is 1.0385, so the standard error is 14.04.

56
00:10:55,662 --> 00:11:06,030
Step 5: the t quantile. With 23 degrees of freedom, the 0.975 quantile is 2.0687.

57
00:11:06,480 --> 00:11:14,629
The half-width is 2.0687 times 14.04, which is 29.04.

58
00:11:15,079 --> 00:11:33,746
The interval is 51.07 plus or minus 29.04. That is 22.0 to 80.1. The orange bar shows it. The tutorial solution, without rounding, gives 22.04 to 80.12.

59
00:11:34,196 --> 00:11:48,212
A second type B example, with the brainhead data of the slides. Pause the video and try it. Predict the brain weight of a new person with head size 3000, with a 95 percent prediction interval.

60
00:11:48,662 --> 00:12:11,681
Step 1 of card B: the values. n is 236. From the data, x bar is 3637.86 and S x x is 30647234. The slides give beta 0 hat, beta 1 hat, and sigma hat equal to 72.35.

61
00:12:12,131 --> 00:12:34,381
Step 2: y new hat is 335.4323 plus 0.2608207 times 3000. That is 335.4323 plus 782.4621, which is 1117.89 grams.

62
00:12:34,831 --> 00:12:55,930
Step 3: the x term. 3000 minus 3637.86, squared, is 406865. Divide by 30647234, and the x term is 0.0133.

63
00:12:56,380 --> 00:13:14,919
The bracket is 1, plus 1 over 236, which is 0.0042, plus 0.0133. That is 1.0175. With n equal to 236, the 1 is almost all of the bracket.

64
00:13:15,369 --> 00:13:31,731
Step 4: the standard error is 72.35 times the square root of 1.0175. That is 72.35 times 1.0087, which is 72.98.

65
00:13:32,181 --> 00:13:42,421
Step 5: with 234 degrees of freedom, the 0.975 quantile of t is 1.970.

66
00:13:42,871 --> 00:13:51,383
The half-width is 1.970 times 72.98, which is 143.8 grams.

67
00:13:51,833 --> 00:14:08,260
The 95 percent prediction interval is 1117.89 plus or minus 143.8. That is 974.1 to 1261.7 grams.

68
00:14:08,710 --> 00:14:19,782
Section 4. Question type C asks you to compare a confidence interval with a prediction interval, to explain their widths, or to decide if you can trust a prediction.

69
00:14:20,232 --> 00:14:35,677
Step 1 of method card C: name the target. The mean mu 0 is a fixed number, so it needs a confidence interval. One new y is random, so it needs a prediction interval. The green band joins the confidence intervals.

70
00:14:36,127 --> 00:14:51,231
Step 2: both intervals have the same centre and the same t quantile. The prediction interval has an extra 1 under the square root, from epsilon new. So it is wider. The orange lines join the prediction intervals.

71
00:14:51,681 --> 00:15:08,300
Step 3: both are narrowest at x 0 equal to x bar. They get wider as x 0 moves away from x bar, because of the term x 0 minus x bar squared over S x x. The red bars show the edges.

72
00:15:08,750 --> 00:15:17,859
Step 4: for a large n, the confidence interval width goes to 0. The prediction interval width does not, because the 1 stays.

73
00:15:18,309 --> 00:15:28,805
Step 5: check that x 0 is inside the range of the observed x. Outside this range, the prediction is an extrapolation. Do not trust it.

74
00:15:29,255 --> 00:15:46,941
Now a tutorial question of type C. Pause the video and try it. Tutorial 2, question 1 d, asks for a CI for the mean satisfaction at severity 55. Part e asks for a PI for one new patient at severity 55.

75
00:15:47,391 --> 00:16:04,415
Step 1 of card C: name the target. Part d is about the mean of all patients with severity 55, a fixed number. So it is a confidence interval. Part e is about one new patient, a random value. So it is a prediction interval.

76
00:16:04,865 --> 00:16:28,395
Step 2: both have the centre 51.07 and the t quantile 2.0687. The only difference is the 1 in the bracket. For the CI, the bracket is 1 over 25 plus 0.0384, which is 0.0784. For the PI, it is 1.0784.

77
00:16:28,845 --> 00:16:47,277
The CI standard error is 13.52 times the square root of 0.0784. The root is 0.28, so it is 3.79. The PI standard error is 14.04, from the last example.

78
00:16:47,727 --> 00:17:03,663
The CI half-width is 2.0687 times 3.79, which is 7.84. So the 95 percent CI is 43.2 to 58.9. The green bar shows it.

79
00:17:04,113 --> 00:17:27,665
The PI half-width is 2.0687 times 14.04, which is 29.04. So the PI is 22.0 to 80.1, the orange bar. 29.04 over 7.84 is 3.7. One patient varies much more than the mean of many patients.

80
00:17:28,115 --> 00:17:39,827
Lecture 4 asks three questions about these bands, on slides 24 and 39. Pause the video and answer them. Here are the brainhead data with the fitted line.

81
00:17:40,277 --> 00:17:49,259
Card C, step 1. The green band joins the 95 percent confidence intervals for the mean, at a grid of x values.

82
00:17:49,709 --> 00:18:05,303
Question 2: why do most points fall outside the green band? The red points are outside it. There are 195 of them. The CI is for the mean, not for one person. One person also has the error epsilon.

83
00:18:05,753 --> 00:18:21,753
Question 3, step 2 of the card. The orange lines join the 95 percent prediction intervals. They are much wider, because of the extra 1. 226 of the 236 points are inside them.

84
00:18:22,203 --> 00:18:42,555
Question 1, step 3 of the card. Both bands are narrowest at x bar, 3638. The term x 0 minus x bar squared over S x x grows toward the edges. The red bars show the wider intervals at the edges. So the lines are not parallel.

85
00:18:43,005 --> 00:19:05,192
This plot shows the half-width of each interval as a function of x 0. The green curve is for the CI. At x bar, the x term is 0. So the half-width is t times sigma hat over root n. 1.970 times 72.35 over 15.36 is 9.3 grams.

86
00:19:05,642 --> 00:19:26,015
The orange curve is for the PI. At x bar, the half-width is t times sigma hat times the square root of 1 plus 1 over n. 1.970 times 72.35 times 1.0021 is 142.8 grams.

87
00:19:26,465 --> 00:19:40,609
Card C, step 3. The term x 0 minus x bar squared, over S x x, is 0 at x bar and grows as x 0 moves away. So both curves have their minimum at x bar.

88
00:19:41,059 --> 00:20:04,078
Step 4. For a large n, 1 over n and the x term go to 0. So the CI half-width goes to 0. The PI half-width goes to t times sigma hat, here 1.970 times 72.35, which is 142.5 grams. More data cannot remove epsilon new.

89
00:20:04,528 --> 00:20:19,355
Now two questions about trust. Pause the video and try them. Tutorial 2, question 1 f: should we trust a prediction for a new patient with severity 100? The top line shows the severity axis.

90
00:20:19,805 --> 00:20:32,263
Step 5 of card C: check the range of the observed x. The tutorial solution says that the largest severity in the data is 62. x bar is 50.48.

91
00:20:32,713 --> 00:20:44,276
Severity 100 is far above 62. This is an extrapolation. The data say nothing about the line at 100, so we should not trust the prediction.

92
00:20:44,726 --> 00:20:57,654
Assignment 1, question 3 e, asks the same thing for berries. In the data, sugar goes from 176.5 to 258.5 grams per litre.

93
00:20:58,104 --> 00:21:13,741
110 grams per litre is far below 176.5. The formula still gives an interval, but it assumes that the line holds at 110. The data cannot check this. So we mistrust the interval.

94
00:21:14,191 --> 00:21:33,839
What does 95 percent mean for a prediction interval? This is our own simulation. We use the brainhead fit as the true model and make 20 new data sets. For each data set, the orange bar is its 95 percent prediction interval at x equal to 4200.

95
00:21:34,289 --> 00:21:46,172
For each data set, we also make one new response at x equal to 4200. The dots are these new responses. A blue dot is inside its interval.

96
00:21:46,622 --> 00:21:50,526
The red dot, in data set 4, is outside its interval.

97
00:21:50,976 --> 00:22:07,403
So 19 of the 20 intervals contain their new y. Over many repetitions, 95 percent of the intervals contain the new response. Card C, step 1: the target y new is random, and the interval is random too.

98
00:22:07,853 --> 00:22:25,538
To recap. Card A derives the prediction interval. The prediction error has mean 0. Its two parts are independent, so its variance is sigma squared times 1 plus 1 over n plus the x term. Standardize, use t, and solve for y new.

99
00:22:25,988 --> 00:22:37,273
Card B computes the interval. Read the values, find y new hat, the bracket, and the standard error. Then y new hat plus or minus t times the standard error.

100
00:22:37,723 --> 00:22:55,579
Card C compares. A mean needs a CI, one new y needs a PI. The PI is wider because of the 1. Both are narrowest at x bar. And check that x new is inside the data. The next video, 10 b, solves more exam problems.
